<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Paul Sartre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Jean-Paul Charles Aymard Sartre, Sartre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Paul Sartre was a French philosopher, political activist and writer, a key figure of existentialism and a leading figure in 20th-century French philosophy and Marxism. His principal philosophical work, Being and Nothingness (1943), centred on the conflict between bad faith and an authentic way of being, and his lecture Existentialism Is a Humanism (1946) introduced his thought. Drafted and captured during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, he co-founded the resistance group Socialisme et Liberté and, after the war, the journal Les Temps modernes. He supported anti-colonial movements and condemned French policies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, and in 1964 was awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature, which he tried to refuse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre.jpg" alt="Jean-Paul Sartre" width="330" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Paul_Sartre_1965.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Senghor followed in 1948 with the Anthologie de la nouvelle poésie nègre et malgache de langue française, which took the manifesto’s role, and its preface by <b>Jean-Paul Sartre</b>, “Orphée Noir” (“Black Orpheus”), carried Négritude into the wider intellectual conversation.</blockquote><a href="/movement/negritude">Négritude · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>…Acemoglu, Johnson and Robinson trace present differences in wealth and government to colonial institutions, and the word neocolonialism, used by <b>Jean-Paul Sartre</b> in 1956 and by Kwame Nkrumah, Ghana’s first president, in Neo-Colonialism: The Last Stage of Imperialism (1965), names economic and political control that outlasted formal empire.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In France students of Louis Althusser formed a Maoist circle in 1964, and the Proletarian Left drew in <b>Jean-Paul Sartre</b> and Michel Foucault; in the United States Maoists became the largest and most dynamic branch of socialism after 1968, and the Black Panther Party quoted Mao liberally.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
